INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (6372, '2d42ee1a8f', 'How I Made The bitPimps Truck', 'Here\'s a semi-quick and dirty explination of how I built the bitPimps truck out of a Bb body.\r\n\r\nFirst I took an x-acto knife to the body, I cut out the roof piece, the back two passenger windows and the very back window - all in one piece. It seemed to make more sense and easier to cut it out in one piece.\r\n\r\nThe idea here is that you can make a some-what sloppy cut to get the main portion out and smooth it out afterward. Just remember your lines when you make your cuts.\r\n[b:2d42ee1a8f]For cutting on the top:[/b:2d42ee1a8f]\r\nYou want to follow from where the front driver door ends and the rear driver door begins, across the top of the car, to where the front passenger door ends and the rear passenger door begins. Don\'t cut down the side of the body past where the windows meet the sides of the car.\r\n[b:2d42ee1a8f]For cutting on the sides:[/b:2d42ee1a8f]\r\nYou want to follow the line where the window meets the sides of the car, follow straight back to the rear of the car. Keep cutting all the way around, again keeping in mind where your final line is going to be, don\'t cut below it.\r\n\r\nNow, you should have a nice chunk taken off the suv body.\r\nNext your going to want to cut the back window off this chunk.\r\nCut on the roof of the suv chunk just before the body molding starts to bend downward and meets the rear window. Smooth out the piece with your knife or really fine grit sanding swatches. Make sure your the edges you cut on are smooth, even, and straight.\r\nSmooth out the cuts you\'ve made on the body in the same way. Once you have all your edges smoothed, clean the car and glue the rear window to the body, just after the front driver and passenger doors.\r\nNow it\'s time to move on to making a cover for the bed.\r\n\r\nI used a piece of plastic from the cover of the packaging when you buy a MS or Tomy car. Cut out a little square that\'s just a little bigger than what you\'ll need to cover the bed of the truck, it\'s easier to work with.\r\nPut it on top of the bed of the truck, making sure it meets the rear window of the cab of the truck. Get your line by using an x-acto knife, running it down the bed of the truck where the cover of the bed meets the sides of the bed. After getting your lines, cut it out - it\'s okay if you have a few small edges hanging over, just make sure they\'re not big and that they don\'t inside the bed of the truck. Glue it down.\r\n\r\nNow, you\'re probably still seeing gaps and lines and two wholes on the top of the cab of the truck where the suv luggage rack bars used to be.\r\nGet some modelers body putty and fill in the holes and fill in around the edges of everything you cut and glued together. Let it dry.\r\n\r\nNext, you\'re going to need super fine grit sanding swatches. They\'re made for sanding on lexan bodies and are such fine grit it feels like notebook paper. Put your body under some running water and start wet sanding the puttied areas, sand everything down until it\'s smooth and you see no lines. When finished, dry it off, let it sit and dry to make sure all the water is gone.\r\n\r\nMask up the body on any areas you plan on leaving clear. When finished, hit the car with a light coat of primer. This will reveal any other tell tale lines left from putting the pieces together. If you followed the steps previously and did a good job, you won\'t see any lines. If however, you do see lines, that\'s okay too - you\'ve got 1 more shot to get it right. Apply putty again to the areas in question. Let it dry, wet sand it. Now examine it very closely, this is your last chance to get it the way you want it. If you notice any edges, lines, gaps, etc. - putty it again and wet sand it again. Keep doing this until you have it looking the way you want. Now hit it with primer again and she\'s ready to be painted.\r\n\r\nSorry no pics on this one fellas, hope the explination helps ya out though. Good luck! ;)');
