INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (9405, '69150036f5', 're:', 'Scale Auto (www.scaleauto.com) has a few springs made for the Super G+, G2 and G3 slot cars that work great as suspension springs.\r\n\r\nPart #221 - BSRT Light Pickup Springs (2 pair) - $1.00 \r\nPart #222 - BSRT Stock Medium Pickup Springs (2 pair) - $1.00 \r\nPart #223 - BSRT Heavy Pickup Springs (2 pair) -$1.00 \r\nPart #HT143 - Gold-Plated BSRT #222 Super G+ Pickup Springs (2 pair) $1.50\r\nPart #8802 - Tomy SG+ Pickup Shoe Springs (2 pair)- $1.00');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (9406, 'ba55659403', 'FAQ: How Do I Optimize My Images?', 'First off, lets understand the process we\'ll be using here:\r\n- you take a picture with your camera, any size, ratio, etc.\r\n- you pull the picture off your camera and on to your computer.\r\n- you optimize your image.\r\n- you re-save your optimized image.\r\n- you upload your image.\r\n- you post your image.\r\n\r\nNow, lets take a look at what we\'ll need to do to optimize our images.\r\n- once your pictures are on your computer, open up your image editing program, I suggest Adobe Photoshop/ImageReady from 4.0 to 6.0. There are many other image editing applications out there, but Photoshop is the best. Fireworks is another commonly used image optimizing program.\r\n- open your picture in your image editing program. crop out the un-neccessary parts of the photo. (i.e. a picture of your bit - we don\'t need to see the keyboard and coke can in the background, just your bit)\r\n- now that your image is cropped and showing only the "subject" of the image, we\'ll probably want to re-size the image. Usually a good width that provides good detail while remaining curtious to other viewers is no larger than 500 pixels wide, 640 pixels wide is the absolute max. You should shoot for 200 pixels - to 500 pixels in width. Any larger and your image sizes will increase.\r\n- now we have our image sized, our aspect ratio is good to go and we\'re almost done. Now, if you\'re using Adobe Photoshop, click "File &gt; Save For Web", when the new window pops up, select JPG or GIF, whichever gives you the smallest file size while retaining the image quality. You can change the optimization settings to be better photo quality or smaller file size or all points in between.\r\n- save the new, newly optimized file.\r\n- upload.\r\n- post.\r\n\r\n\r\nthat\'s it, the quick and dirty version.\r\nwhenever I get more time I\'ll post a more in depth article on this with pretty pictures  :-D');
