INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (15254, '46f01a9259', '', '[quote:46f01a9259="noxorc"]cool. good job...\r\n\r\ntwo questions..\r\n\r\nwhere do you get the 6v from?\r\n[/quote:46f01a9259]\r\n\r\nThe 6V is the circuit voltage that was given with the schematic.  It doesn\'t need to be this.  I will be running it off 5V using a DCDC Converter.  This will run off the standard 1.2V battery that\'s in the car.   I will be using a very low power version of these IC\'s, the TLC555 and CD4017 chips which are both CMOS (meaning ultra-low power drain).   In fact, the LEDs will drain more current than the IC\'s.\r\n\r\n[quote:46f01a9259="noxorc"]how do I interpret the black arrow going in the middle of the 500k resistor?\r\n[/quote:46f01a9259]\r\n\r\nAs Type said the 500k resistor is a potentiometer (or variable resistor) and the black arrow corresponds to the where you have turned the shaft and what resistance is there.   In the video posted earlier you can see as I turn the pot (top middle of the screen) the LEDs cycle a lot faster.  I\'m toying with the idea of leaving this in the final circuit or doing away with it and putting a fixed resistor in it\'s place.  I think variable speed would be cool though.  It\'s a matter of space.\r\n\r\nAlso is the problem of turning it on &amp; off.  I hate having huge switches sticking out of my bit\'s but!  I might just leave it on all the time........\r\n\r\nThis is a "spare time" project whilst I\'m waiting for some more parts to arrive for my H-Bridge project.\r\n\r\nph2t.');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (15255, '60a299d1a7', '', '[quote:60a299d1a7="hogjowlz"]what about usb?[/quote:60a299d1a7]\r\n\r\nA USB mouse does offer a faster sample rate, definately more than PS2 &amp; serial, but I think the issue that Nox is stating is that the sampling rate is limited by the driver software used by the OS for the mouse interface.\r\n\r\nAgain, *I think* :???: .  Nox will correct me I\'m sure.\r\n \r\nph2t.');
