INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (17582, '68e321e02c', '', 'aiight pimps, here\'s my awesome speed mod for your slow-ass zips... man, this took a long time to do! lol\r\n\r\n[b:68e321e02c]Internal Dual Cell Mod For Zipzaps[/b:68e321e02c]\r\n\r\n[u:68e321e02c]supplies[/u:68e321e02c]:\r\n\r\nzipzap\r\n2 NiMH/ NiCd batts\r\nsoldering iron (with solder)\r\nelectrical tape\r\nsandpaper\r\nextra wire\r\nwire strippers/razor knife\r\n*paper towel\r\n*clamp, should look like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19450453][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url]\r\n\r\n*=unnecessary\r\n\r\n1. First off, you need to set up your work area. Start by laying down your paper towel. then, place your clamp(if you\'re using one) on the towel. the clamp is there to keep stuff in place without you having to burn yourself lol. I definitely recommend this or something like it. Plug your soldering gun in and set it on the paper towel as well.\r\n\r\n2. The next step is to prep your car. start off by taking off the clear plastic PCB cover. do this carefully by pushing backwards and pulling the plastic tabs in the back of the cover. once this is off, carefully pull your PCB chip on the driver side to the right. it should create a right angle. take out your battery using either your fingernail or screwdriver. now set the chassis on your paper towel.\r\n\r\n3. take your batteries and sand the terminals on both the postive and negative sides, to make them scratchy. this will help the solder apply better. cut a piece of wire about and inch and a half long and strip the ends about 3/8"with your wire strippers of razor knife.\r\n\r\n4. Place a battery positive side up in your clamp. add a little bit of solder to the tip of your soldering iron and grab your wire. place the wire on the battery terminal and hold your soldering iron down onto the battery. it may take a while, but the solder should eventually stick to the battery and the wire. pull up on the soldering iron and the the solder harden.\r\n\r\n5. Now that you have a wire on this battery, you need to put another on, on the negative terminal. Do the same thing as step 5, but make sure that you angle the wire the same way as the first.\r\n\r\nYour batt should look something like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19456862][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url]\r\n\r\n6. next, you need to put your other battery in your clamp, negative terminal up. This time, take your wire from your positive terminal on your other battery and solder it onto the negative terminal of your second batt. \r\n\r\nNow, your batteries should be connected with one wire left, looking like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19457167][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url]\r\n\r\n7. The next step is one of the most difficult tasks of internal dual celling. Place the chassis in the clamp. Solder the wire from the negative terminal of the first batt to some place on the negative contact on the chassis. It should look like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19457864][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url]\r\n\r\n8. The next step is to get the negative terminal on the chassis out of the way. Take a screwdriver and carefully push down the piece of metal that makes contact with the negative terminal on the battery normally. It should look like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19457401][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url], only with a battery applied to the terminal.\r\n\r\n9. now, you have to tape up the negative terminal of your batt that has the wire attached to it from the other batt. Using electrical tape, simply put a few layers on top of the terminal and solder/wire.\r\n\r\n10. now you need to put the batteries in the car for good. On one battery, you should have a “vacant” positive terminal, and a wire from the negative terminal to your other battery’s positive terminal. On that battery, you should have a wire from the negative terminal to the negative contact of the chassis. Once this is correct, you can now put your batteries in the car! Start by carefully putting in the batt with two wires in first, so it looks like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19458360][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url]. Then, you can put in your second battery. The positive “vacant” terminal should touch the positive contact of that chassis. It should be a tight fit. If it is not, add more electrical tape to the opposite side.\r\n\r\nWhen you’ve finished this step, it should look like [url=http://www.pbase.com/image/19458597][b:68e321e02c][color=red:68e321e02c]this[/color:68e321e02c][/b:68e321e02c][/url].\r\n\r\n11. Your final step is to put the pcb chip back on. Carefully turn it back to its original position. It will not be level with the top of the chassis. Take a piece of electrical tape about 2 inches long or so and apply it tightly to the top of the chip. It should go at least all the way down the side of the chassis.\r\n\r\nyour finished product should look something like this:\r\n\r\n[img:68e321e02c]http://www.rcpics.net/media/internal_dual_cell_zip.jpg[/img:68e321e02c]\r\nThank you....\r\n\r\n~midget_man1387~\r\n\r\n[b:68e321e02c]Edit:[/b:68e321e02c] [i:68e321e02c](CaboWabo)[/i:68e321e02c]\r\njust bolded your links so they were easier to see');
