INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (38093, '0ec169b45d', '', '[quote:0ec169b45d="cyborgzero"]Crazydave, \r\n\r\nSure, you can post a full tutorial, thats fine by me.\r\n\r\nAs far as how it compares to uAs, I have no idea, but, judging from the videos of his cars I have seen, its identical if not really close..\r\n\r\nNot like he is gonna come forth and tell us or anything lol..\r\n\r\nThats why I made up the hillclimb test.. To have something less *subjective" than speed.. There are so many things that can change the speed of a bit, but, the hillclimb really gives you a good idea of what is going on, and, regardless of the gearing and such, you can get up close enough to actually see how fast the wheels are moving and what angle is involved..\r\n\r\nI think the best way to do the hillclimb looking back on it would have been to use some rough grit sandpaper on the surface of the incline..\r\n\r\nI will prlly post another with sandpaper later on to decrease wheelspin, like maybe 220 or 300 grit would be good..\r\n\r\nRob[/quote:0ec169b45d]\r\n\r\nJust some tips on maximum traction that I have found helpful. \r\n\r\nFor softening and cleaning the tires, making them stick to the road, I find that letting them sit in hot water for 20-30 seconds can soften them a lot, and wiping/squeezing them dry with a lint-free napkin or towel will help greatly. \r\n\r\nFor the surface, don\'t use sandpaper, that may cause the tires to rip and wear out a LOT quicker. Instead, make sure you have a HARD, smooth, flat surface like plastic or wood or something, and make sure to wipe all the dust off it, wash it and clean it (like mopping, or whatever), and your tires will grip that road and never let go.....unless you pick it up.  :-)');
