INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (26969, '7a55b85b44', '', '[quote:7a55b85b44="CheeseWarfare"]Ahh you accually made the Savannah look good nice job dave.  I was thinking would it help lowering some to remove the springs? Or are they stuck in place hard?[/quote:7a55b85b44]I personally think the springs are too hard, because they don\'t compress at all when the car is on the ground. Could be why all my bodies sat a little high in the front.\r\n\r\nMaybe we could starting cutting coils out of the springs. Be sounding like the full size guys, "I cut 1 1/12 coils out to lower the front end."\r\n\r\nBut I\'ll wait until I have another SE before I try any experiments like that. Lowering the tab is good enough for now.');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (26970, '730b812f60', '', 'A few tips and reasons:\r\n\r\n1) The higher the frequency of the tx/rx the better range you get out of the box, period.   This is because at a higher freq energy radiates futher for the same amount of power.   \r\n\r\n2) Clone tx\'s have a better range than MS/Bit-g tx\'s cause the circuit is a bit different in the RF output stage on the controller.   The rx pcb of a clone is usually the same as the original MS/Bit-g\'s pcb\'s so that\'s why hog is correct is saying that the tomy controllers suck ass.\r\n\r\n3) A longer antenna on the car will get you better performance versus increasing the antenna length on the controller, normalised.\r\n\r\n4) The more right angled to the ground the antenna is (IE: it\'s as straight up as possible) the better range you wil get.  This is because the antenna works better when the ground plane is at even density around it.  (RF theory states this as a fundamental requirement when dealing wih antennas that are [b:730b812f60]NOT[/b:730b812f60] a full wavelength long for the freqency at hand).   For 27Mhz a full antenna is 11M is length!\r\n\r\nI use a number 9 electric guitar string and it works very well.   The avg length I use is approx 12cm.   I can get an avg distance of 11M indoors and over 20m outside.\r\n\r\nph2t.');
