INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (37181, '169f946508', '', 'Wheel mods on a wild charge truck is simple, the hardest thing is findin\' the right wheels and getting them to glue on center.  That was the problem I had.  I used the wheels off of a Hot Wheels Turnerz Truck.  \r\nCheck out the pics....\r\n\r\n[url]http://www.angelfire.com/bug/sg219[/url]\r\n\r\nThe only way your getting more bodies for that is to do some serious moddin\', because I haven\'t found a damn thing that fits conveintly.\r\n\r\nYes you can change the motors in them, just like bits and zaps...\r\n\r\nAs far as doing any other mods, you can change the battery, that seems pretty simple to do.  And for gearing, thats a whole different ball game, open it up and see.  Your on your own on that one, since it has about 50 gears in it, because of the steering it uses and the torque.');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (32831, 'b2d1abe6bc', 'Week #15', 'Owner: [b:b2d1abe6bc]DriveWRX[/b:b2d1abe6bc]\r\n[b:b2d1abe6bc]30-Nov-03[/b:b2d1abe6bc]\r\n \r\n[img:b2d1abe6bc]http://bitpimps.lixlink.com/pages/phpGallery/albums/userpics/10001/15_drivewrx_1.jpg[/img:b2d1abe6bc]');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (32903, 'c35be20503', '', 'Ok, here we go. Get ready, \'cause it took some time for me to put this together.\r\n\r\nFirst before I get into my bit mods I wanted to show you one of my scratch built pedals for my guitar. So you realize I didn\'t just jump into this with no experience\r\n\r\n[img:c35be20503]http://s2k-kidz.com/crazycars/fuzz.jpg[/img:c35be20503]\r\nThis is the third pedal I had built. What I did here is take a schematic of an existing circuit, breadboard it. Swapped some resistors, and capacitors to tune the tone to my particular guitar and amp setup. Prototyped a circuit, and etched the circuit board. Then I soldered it altogether into the mess you see on the right. It aint pretty, but it works.\r\n\r\nMy very first micro RC electronic mod I did was to my first Zip Zap. I removed a couple resistors, and jumpered them to get a slight improvement from the weak first generation ZZ PCBs. While a slight improvement, it wasn\'t good enough. So I decided to do a PCB swap. First I started with guts from a Motorworks, but that screwed up my steering, and I didn\'t care for being stuck with one motor, with the motor hardwired in. So I gutted it, and it layed stripped for a month or so.\r\n\r\nThen I built it into what I considered the ultimate car at the time. The one I called Franken-Zap. Behold:\r\n[img:c35be20503]http://s2k-kidz.com/crazycars/frank7.jpg[/img:c35be20503]\r\n\r\nI swapped in a PCB from a Microsizer Sport, and tri-celled it. I was running a fairly quick motor from a Cannonball clone, and 9.8:1 gearing. It was a awsome car, but had a tendency to end up on it\'s roof. \r\n\r\nFranken-Zap died a dramatic, and untimely death when a patched up steering wire shorted on the batteries, blowing a transistor off the board, and causing a tiny fire, and lots of smoke. \r\n\r\nMonths later I was actually able to repair the smoked PCB, but in the meantime I decided to go a different direction with Franken-Zap. Here\'s how it stands now.\r\n[img:c35be20503]http://s2k-kidz.com/crazycars/frank8.jpg[/img:c35be20503]\r\n\r\nDon\'t look like much is going on does it? That\'s the way I wanted it. I decided to go about building a car that was just as fast, more controllable, and appeared stock.\r\n\r\nHere\'s what I got going on here. A FET modded LXX booster board, w/ the Anti-Boost mod done to the controller. Dual Ni-Cds, because they have a  lower internal resistance, and punch better than Ni-MHs. With the juice flowing more effeciently, I\'m able to set it up for a lot more speed. So it\'s running a Bit Char-G Motors pink endbell 3.8, red ZZ gears, and tall Motorworks rear tires, which all combined to turn that extra power into higher speeds. \r\n\r\nI\'m not sure because I\'ve never made a direct comparison, but I swear this car is now as fast if not faster than in it\'s tri-cell form, and all that combined with a drastically more controllable car. I\'m particularly fond of this car, because it\'s my first, and it\'s now my fastest. \r\n\r\nHere\'s one I did for someone else, that I didn\'t paint the PCB cover, so you can get a better idea of what\'s going on.\r\n[img:c35be20503]http://s2k-kidz.com/crazycars/vdmszz.jpg[/img:c35be20503]\r\n\r\nMy most recent accomplishment has been figureing out how to FET the Thunder Charger PCB. Which looks like a Microsizer PCB at first glance, but is actually laid out totally different.\r\n[img:c35be20503]http://s2k-kidz.com/crazycars/tcfet.jpg[/img:c35be20503]\r\n\r\nThis was important to me, because you take a five dolla clone, that already comes with a reasonably fast motor, add a couple dollas worth a FETs, and you have the micro RC bargain of the year.\r\n\r\nRight now I\'m working on the ZZ SE. I\'ve had already found the 2 N-channel transistors on the bottom, and replaced those with stacked FETs. Then I later found the 2 P-channel ones on top of the PCB. I replaced those with flipped N-channel FETs, and figured out how to jumper them. While this has improved performance, but it still doesn\'t act like a FET modded car. I would be happy as is, but micro-amps has managed to figure out a solution, so that keeps me working at it. Ph2t has figured out how to get the full 2.6v flowing to the motor by using flipped P-channel FETs in the N-channel spots, and jumpering to the VE+ rail. I just ordered my new batch of FETs today, so hopefully by next week I will have the news on that. If it works as well as I\'m hoping, then that will be good enough, and I can move on to upgrading the FETs in my Xmod. Which will be no prob, because it\'s already been posted how to do that.\r\n\r\nWhew, so there it, that\'s where I stand with bit electronics.');
