INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (45794, '5854a45b14', 'Shhhh!!! Sneak Peak 4 Help....', 'Allright, here is part of my entry to the contest.  I really wanted to keep it on the DL, but I need some help!!  And of course the pic sucks, but it gives you an idea.\r\n\r\n[img:5854a45b14]http://img28.photobucket.com/albums/v85/sg219/RC%20Pimp%20Stuff/contest%20land%20and%20sea/summerride.jpg[/img:5854a45b14]\r\n\r\nIts a ZZ chassis thats seriously chopped up, a slot car motor, NKOK PCB board, steering coils from something, and all custom battery terminals set up for internal dual cell (2--60mah cells).....\r\n\r\nI need to get the motor going, then my next project is the steering!!!\r\n\r\nWell thats all I\'m tellin\' now.....\r\n\r\n\r\nHere is my problem......\r\n\r\nThe motor isn\'t working with the board.  I don\'t think the board lets enough power thru to reach the motor.  If someone could tell me how to use one of those crazy digital volt meters, I could figure it out maybe. I don\'t know what setting on it to use to check it.  I do have a battery that makes it work when I touch the leads to the motor, but that is direct current, without the board in the way.\r\n\r\nI am not even close to knowing what I\'m doing, but its fun and one damn hell of a challenge!!  Plus, I like trying to solder things I can barely see!!  Especially after a few beers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEdit&gt;&gt;&gt;Also, I should say, the motor came out of a Hot Wheels car with a battery and charging tabs.  Not a Slut car unless its a Hot Wheels Slut Car?  Bought it at the thrift store for 35 cents...I\'ll edit the original post.');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (45795, 'e7751ef884', '', '[quote:e7751ef884="viperdout"]At school at at home, it\'s S2 Drag with soft slicks (haven\'t tried my new tires yet since my X is in pieces), and I can do perfect drifts with a stock car and fairly slippery floors.[/quote:e7751ef884]\r\n\r\nSome drift techniques can come from "throttle steering". This can come from turning slightly, then gunning the throttle and losing traction, then releasing the steering wheel. The thing is, the loss of traction and momentum gained from jerking the rear out ([size=7:e7751ef884]that\'s what she said[/size:e7751ef884]) will push the rear further if the rear still has a loss of traction, even if the front wheels are dead on straight.\r\n\r\nHere\'s a good example of "throttle steer":\r\n\r\nhttp://www.kteamtrivia.co.uk/Motorsport/Videos/videos.html\r\n\r\nCheck out the second vid, from top to bottom. Save the video, and watch after it says "In case you missed it, here it is again in slow motion". Notice how that car\'s front wheels are pointed straight while the car is drifting, hence the term "throttle steer". Ever see someone in a wheel chair? If they power the wheels themselves with their hands, they are turning one wheel forward and the other backward, turning themselves. This principle is applied to the front wheels in "throttle steer", and that\'s how the car safely turns sharply without spinning out...that car in the vid is an FR (front engine, rear wheel drive) car. When that driver wanted to zoom straight out of the turn, he reduced the throttle, or gave the rear wheels less torque (too much torque in wheel rotation causes wheelspin) by either shifting up to a less-torquey gear (dunno whether it\'s called "higher gear" or "lower gear"), or by taking his foot off the gas.\r\n\r\nThat\'s a good technique to use with RWD Xmods. Just turn sharply for a split second, and since the wheels in the back have more rpm, they have a little bit of slip. Turning sharply slows down the speed of the car a tad bit, and jerks a bit of weight forward, and this allows the rear to slip out a bit...but since the wheels are turned only for a split second, the car doesn\'t spin out so easily. I remember micromatch telling me that with his Mini-Z, he turned the wheel quickly 5 times to produce a nice drift.\r\n\r\nPowersliding is not to be confused with drifting. Powersliding is just whipping the butt of the car around the corner. \r\n\r\nIf any of this info is wrong, feel free to correct.');
INSERT INTO phpbb_posts_text VALUES (45796, '143f9e1a2a', '', 'You know what voltage the motor is rated for? A lot of slot car motors are designed to run at 12v.\r\n\r\nCheck out Actofgod\'s BFM-9000 mod over at TRC.\r\nhttp://tinyrc.com/forums/showthread.php?s=&amp;threadid=10084&amp;highlight=bfm\r\n\r\nThe motor he\'s using looks just like the one in Nikko\'s little trucks.');
